Several kinds of CEP processes can be distinguished. First, the central state can be produced in the fully photon-induced interaction,, also called double photon exchange. Because of the electromagnetic coupling being roughly two orders of magnitude smaller than, this CEP mechanism is suitable for studies in or in ultra peripheral heavy-ion collisions. The photon flux factor, proportional to the product of squared atomic numbers of interacting nuclei, can then enhance the cross section to the measurable level.

The second possibility is the photoproduction process. In this process, one of the beam particles is a source of a photon, typically of low virtuality, while the other a source of a Reggeon, typically the Pomeron. As a result a vector meson is produced, e.g. or.

The third possibility is an interaction of the fully strong nature, in the high energy limit - the DPE. At values below these achievable at the LHC, the exchanges of secondary Reggeons can also

