Exactly the same cross-check was done for \(n\sigma_{X}\) variables, which are directly used to identify particles in physics analysis. They carry information about the distance (in width units) of reconstructed \(dE/dx\) from the MPV of \(dE/dx\) according to Bichsel parametrization - they are kind of a pull variables. For every track \(n\sigma_{X}\) was recalculated using its definition:

\[n\sigma_{X}=\left(\ln\frac{dE^{\prime}/dx}{dE/dx_{X}^{\rm exp.}}\right)\bigg{|} \sigma_{dE/dx}\;, \tag{8.8}\]

where instead of original \(dE/dx\) the adjusted one (\(dE^{\prime}/dx\)) was used. Uncertainty of reconstructed \(dE/dx\) was left unchanged. The sample comparison of \(n\sigma_{\rm pion}\), \(n\sigma_{\rm kaon}\) and \(n\sigma_{\rm proton}\) variables can be found in Fig. 8.8. As in the case of \(dE/dx\) comparison, in the lower panels of Fig. 8.8 the ratios of the MC to the data also demonstrate better agreement of the MC and the data, which was the goal of the adjustment.

