particle. In the earlier analysis of diffractive events in Ref. [33], it was found, that this nominal matching definition results sometimes in matching two tracks to the same particle. Therefore, an additional requirement on the angular distance in the \(\left(\eta,\phi\right)\) plane, between the track and the particle, \(\delta\left(\eta,\phi\right)\), was introduced. It was defined through relation

\[\delta^{2}\left(\eta,\phi\right)=\left(\eta^{\text{true}}-\eta^{\text{reco}} \right)^{2}+\left(\phi^{\text{true}}-\phi^{\text{reco}}\right)^{2}. \tag{11.5}\]

Distribution of \(\delta^{2}\left(\eta,\phi\right)\) for pions is shown in Fig. 11.5a. Based on the distribution, a cut on \(\delta^{2}\left(\eta,\phi\right)<0.15^{2}\) was applied in the matching procedure in addition to the nominal STAR requirement on the number of hits. It has been checked that this cut value is also appropriate for kaons and (anti-)protons. The influence of making the matching requirement more rigorous on the TPC efficiency is shown in Figs. 11.5b, 11.5c and 11.5d, for different particle species, as a function of \(p_{\text{T}}\). Differences between the two approaches are not very significant, except for the efficiency for \(\bar{p}\) at very low \(p_{\text{T}}\). The extended matching definition helped to reduce an artificial effect of large TPC reconstruction efficiency for anti-protons of low transverse momentum, resulting from the high sensitivity of \(\bar{p}\) to the interactions with the inactive material (annihilation on the beam pipe).

