#### TPC \(z\)-vertex cut (SC2)

Removing from analysis specific range of \(z\)-positions of primary vertices effectively reduced accepted luminosity with respect to that delivered by the collider. This loss of luminosity must be accounted for when calculating the cross sections. Assuming that the distribution of \(z_{\rm vtx}\) is normal (\(\mathcal{N}\)), then the efficiency of the cut SC2 is equal to:

\[\epsilon_{z_{\rm vtx}}=\frac{1}{N}\int\limits_{z_{\rm vtx}^{\rm min}}^{z_{\rm vtx }^{\rm max}}\mathcal{N}(z_{\rm vtx};\mu,\sigma)\;\mathrm{d}z_{\rm vtx}=\frac{1} {2}\left[\mathrm{Erf}\left(\frac{z_{\rm vtx}^{\rm max}-\mu}{\sqrt{2}\sigma} \right)-\mathrm{Erf}\left(\frac{z_{\rm vtx}^{\rm min}-\mu}{\sqrt{2}\sigma} \right)\right], \tag{11.16}\]

where \(N\) is the number of all events, \(z_{\rm vtx}^{\rm min}\) and \(z_{\rm vtx}^{\rm max}\) are respectively minimum and maximum value of the longitudinal position of the vertex accepted in analysis (here: -80 cm and 80 cm), and the parameters \(\mu\) and \(\sigma\) are, respectively, the average position and width of the \(z_{\rm vtx}\) distribution in data: \(\mu=\langle z_{\rm vtx}\rangle\) and \(\sigma=\sigma(z_{\rm vtx})\).

As the parameters vary significantly between RHIC fills (see Fig. 11.20), they were obtained for each fill separately from Gaussian fits, see Fig. 11.19. The efficiency used in the correction procedure was calculated independently for each fill using presented values of \(\langle z_{\rm vtx}\rangle\) and \(\sigma(z_{\rm vtx})\). Typical value of this efficiency equals \((88\pm 2)\%\).

