efficiency was calculated as the ratio

\[\varepsilon_{\mathrm{SpTrk}}^{\mathrm{track}}=\frac{N_{\mathrm{SpTrk}}^{\mathrm{ track}}}{N^{\mathrm{track}}}. \tag{18.8}\]

The result is shown in Fig. 18.5a as a function of \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) of the offline ID track (no significant dependence on track's \(\eta\) was found). Clearly, the efficiency rises rapidly around \(p_{\mathrm{T}}=0.2\) GeV which matches the value of \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) required at the trigger L2. The dashed red line shows the function

\[\varepsilon_{\mathrm{SpTrk}}^{\mathrm{track}}(p_{\mathrm{T}})=\frac{1}{2} \cdot P_{1}\cdot\left[\mathrm{Erf}\Big{(}\frac{p_{\mathrm{T}}-P_{2}}{P_{3}} \Big{)}+1\right] \tag{18.9}\]

fitted to black points. This function fails to describe the \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\)-dependence of the efficiency between \(0.2-0.3\) GeV. Because of this, function from Eq. (18.9) was modified to the form

\[\varepsilon_{\mathrm{SpTrk}}^{\mathrm{track}}(p_{\mathrm{T}})=\frac{1}{2} \cdot P_{1}\cdot\left[\mathrm{Erf}\Big{(}\frac{p_{\mathrm{T}}-P_{2}}{P_{3}} \Big{)}+1\right]+\left\{\begin{array}{ll}0&\mathrm{for}\ p_{\mathrm{T}}<0.3 \ \mathrm{GeV}\\ P_{4}&\mathrm{for}\ p_{\mathrm{T}}\geq 0.3\ \mathrm{GeV}\end{array}\right., \tag{18.10}\]

which differs from the initial form by a discontinuity at \(p_{\mathrm{T}}=0.3\) GeV. Such value emerges from the plot and is also motivated by the correspondence to track's radius of 0.5 m, which matches the radius of the fourth SCT layer (Fig. 13.3b), hence tracks of this (and higher) radii have increased chance for inducing required number of the SCT space points. Dashed blue line in Fig. 18.5a represents a fit of the function from Eq. (18.10) to the data points. The quality of the fit shows that it is a good model for the SpTrk efficiency dependence over track's \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\). It is worth to mention, that the efficiency does not reach 100% and saturates at approximately 98%. It might be a result of some residual fake tracks present in a sample selected for the efficiency determination. Nevertheless, any possible bias is covered by the systematic uncertainty determined in Sec. 19.1.

In physics analysis there are at least two ID tracks, and each of them could have independently satisfied the SpTrk HLT component. For this reason, the efficiency for the entire event is calculated as

\[\varepsilon_{\mathrm{SpTrk}}(p_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{max}},p_{\mathrm{T}}^{ \mathrm{min}})=1-\underbrace{\left(1-\varepsilon_{\mathrm{SpTrk}}^{\mathrm{ track}}(p_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{max}})\right)\cdot\left(1-\varepsilon_{\mathrm{SpTrk}}^{ \mathrm{track}}(p_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{min}})\right)}_{\mathrm{probability}}. \tag{18.11}\]

