## Appendix B Momentum reconstruction using STAR RP system

A method for momentum reconstruction of protons tagged by the RP detectors is presented below. A geometrical approach is used with an assumption of a constant and uniform magnetic field inside the DX magnet.

The RP subsystem (Fig. 5.8), with two vertical RP stations on each side of the STAR IP, allows to measure positions of scattered proton at two locations, 15.8 m and 17.6 m from the IP. From the measured coordinates \((x_{1},y_{1})\) and \((x_{2},y_{2})\) one can reconstruct local angles, \(\theta_{x}^{\text{RP}}\) and \(\theta_{y}^{\text{RP}}\), using simple relations:

\[\tan\theta_{x}^{\text{RP}}=\frac{x_{2}-x_{1}}{d_{\text{RP}}},\qquad\tan\theta_ {y}^{\text{RP}}=\frac{y_{2}-y_{1}}{d_{\text{RP}}},\] (B.1)

where \(d_{\text{RP}}\) is the distance between the RP stations in the same branch.

Assuming that there is a constant and uniform magnetic field inside the DX magnet, \(\vec{B}=(0,-B,0)\), the \(y\)-component of the scattered proton's momentum remains unchanged on its way from the IP to the RP stations, hence the \(y\)-component of the scattered proton's polar angle at the IP, \(\theta_{y}=\theta_{y}^{\text{RP}}\) (Fig. B.2).

However, the trajectory of the proton in the \(xz\) plane is curved when traversing the DX magnet due to the Lorentz force (Figs. B.1 and B.3). The radius of the circular path is given by:

\[R=\frac{p_{xz}}{eB},\] (B.2)

where \(p_{xz}\) is the momentum component in the \(xz\) plane, related to the total momentum, \(p\), by

\[\frac{p_{xz}}{p}=\cos\theta_{y}.\] (B.3)

Introducing the fractional momentum loss of of the scattered proton, \(\xi=(p_{0}-p)/p_{0}\), where \(p_{0}\) is the beam proton momentum, one can express the radius of curvature as:

\[R=\underbrace{\frac{p_{0}}{eB}}_{R_{0}}\cdot\cos\theta_{y}\cdot(1-\xi),\] (B.4)

where \(R_{0}\) is the radius of curvature of the beam trajectory inside the DX dipole. Since maximum \(\theta_{y}\) contained within RP acceptance is expected to be of the order of 5 mrad, factor \(\cos\theta_{y}\) changes \(R\) by 0.001%, hence it can be neglected in further calculations. The \(B\)-field for \(p=100\) GeV is nominally equal to 1.7 T, which yields \(R_{0}=196.2\) m.

In order to find the relation between local angle \(\theta_{x}^{\text{RP}}\) and corresponding angle \(\theta_{x}\) at IP let us use the STAR coordinate system. We assume ideal alignment of the DX magnet with respect to the beam (beam perpendicular to DX at the entrance). Let us write an equation describing the circle with centre at \(S=(d_{1}-\Delta z,x_{\text{IP}}+x_{in}-\Delta x+R)\), whose part is the trajectory of proton inside DX magnet:

\[(z-d_{1}+\Delta z)^{2}+(x-x_{\text{IP}}-x_{in}+\Delta x-R)^{2}=R^{2}.\] (B.5)

Symbols used in Eq. (B.5) are explained in Figs. B.1 and B.3. From above one can find, that for protonin DX magnet

\[x(z)=x_{\rm IP}+x_{in}-\Delta x+R-R\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{z-d_{1}+\Delta z}{R} \right)^{2}}.\] (B.6)

From the derivative of Eq. (B.6),

\[\frac{dx}{dz}=\left.\frac{z-d_{1}+\Delta z}{R}\right/\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{z-d_{1 }+\Delta z}{R}\right)^{2}},\] (B.7)

one can easily find the angle, at which proton leaves the field of magnet DX:

\[\frac{dx}{dz}\bigg{|}_{z=d_{1}+t_{\rm DX}}=\tan\alpha_{p}=\left.\frac{l_{\rm DX }+\Delta z}{R}\right/\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{l_{\rm DX}+\Delta z}{R}\right)^{2}}.\] (B.8)

From Figs. B.1 and B.3 one can find the following relations:

\[\Delta z=R\sin\theta_{x},\qquad\Delta x=R(1-\cos\theta_{x}),\qquad x_{in}=(d_ {1}-z_{\rm IP})\tan\theta_{x}.\] (B.9)

Inserting formulas for \(\Delta x\) and \(R\) to Eq. (B.8) one obtains:

\[\tan\alpha_{p}=\left(\frac{l_{\rm DX}}{R_{0}(1-\xi)}+\sin\theta_{x}\right)/ \sqrt{1-\left(\frac{l_{\rm DX}}{R_{0}(1-\xi)}+\sin\theta_{x}\right)^{2}}\.\] (B.10)

Then, for beam protons (no scattering i.e. \(\theta_{x}=0\), \(\xi=0\), \(\alpha_{p}=\alpha_{0}\)) one obtains

\[\tan\alpha_{0}=\left.\frac{l_{\rm DX}}{R_{0}}\right/\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{l_{\rm DX }}{R_{0}}\right)^{2}}\quad\Rightarrow\quad\left.\alpha_{0}\right|_{p=100\ {\rm GeV}}=18.86\ {\rm mrad}.\] (B.11)

The above value is in perfect agreement with declared value of the bending angle of DX magnet [220].

Figure B.1: Scheme of proton trajectory. Proportions of elements are not preserved.

Since \(l_{\rm DX}\ll R_{0}\) and \(\alpha_{0}\) is small,

\[\alpha_{0}\approx\frac{l_{\rm DX}}{R_{0}}.\] (B.12)

The local angle measured in Roman Pots is related to the bending angle of the trajectory of scattered proton by

\[\alpha_{p}=\alpha_{0}+\theta_{x}^{\rm RP}\] (B.13)

(see Fig. B.3). Simple transformation of Eq. (B.10) and insertion of (B.13) gives formula for \(\theta_{x}\):

\[\sin\theta_{x}=\frac{\tan\left(\alpha_{0}+\theta_{x}^{\rm RP}\right)}{\sqrt{1+ \tan^{2}\left(\alpha_{0}+\theta_{x}^{\rm RP}\right)}}-\frac{l_{\rm DX}}{R_{0} (1-\xi)}.\] (B.14)

For small angles, which are considered here, one can approximate Eq. (B.14) by

\[\theta_{x}\approx\theta_{x}^{\rm RP}-\alpha_{0}\frac{\xi}{1-\xi}.\] (B.15)

As one can notice, for the elastic scattering (\(\xi=0\)), the angle measured in RP system and the initial angle at the vertex, are equal.

To reconstruct \(\theta_{x}\) and \(\xi\), one has to use the position of proton at the face of detector. Using Fig. B.3, one can find the following relations:

\[x_{1}=\widetilde{x}+\left(d_{2}-\widetilde{d}\right)\tan\theta_{x}^{\rm RP}, \hskip 28.452756pt\frac{\widetilde{x}}{\delta x}=\cos\alpha_{0},\hskip 28.452756pt \frac{\widetilde{d}}{\delta x}=\sin\alpha_{0}.\] (B.16)

Combining the above leads to

\[x_{1}=\delta x\left(\cos\alpha_{0}-\sin\alpha_{0}\tan\theta_{x}^{\rm RP} \right)+d_{2}\tan\theta_{x}^{\rm RP}.\] (B.17)

The difference, \(\delta x\), between the \(x\)-positions of the scattered and beam protons, at the exit of DX magnet, can be written as

\[\delta x=x(d_{1}+l_{\rm DX};\theta_{x},\xi)-x_{0},\] (B.18)

where

\[x_{0}=x(d_{1}+l_{\rm DX};0,0)=R_{0}\left[1-\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{l_{\rm DX}}{R_ {0}}\right)^{2}}\right]\approx\frac{l_{\rm DX}^{2}}{2R_{0}}\approx 3.49\ {\rm cm}.\] (B.19)

Merging Eq. (B.18) with (B.6) and (B.19) results in

\[\delta x=x_{\rm IP}+\left(d_{1}-z_{\rm IP}\right)\tan\theta_{x}+R_{0}(1-\xi) \left[\cos\theta_{x}-\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{l_{\rm DX}}{R_{0}(1-\xi)}+\sin \theta_{x}\right)^{2}}\right]-R_{0}\left[1-\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{l_{\rm DX}}{R_ {0}}\right)^{2}}\right].\] (B.20)

All the components of Eq. (B.17) are now represented in the form dependent on \(\theta_{x}\) or \(\theta_{x}^{\rm RP}\), hence one can easily derive dependencies between position of scattered proton in the detector \(x_{1}\) and the scattering angle at the vertex, \(\theta_{x}\), fractional momentum loss, \(\xi\), or the angle in the RP system, \(\theta_{x}^{\rm RP}\):

\[x_{1}=x_{\rm IP}+\left(d_{1}+l_{\rm DX}-z_{\rm IP}\right)\theta_{x}+d_{2} \theta_{x}^{\rm RP}+\frac{l_{\rm DX}^{2}}{2R_{0}}\cdot\frac{\xi}{1-\xi},\] (B.21)

\[\theta_{x}=\frac{x_{1}-x_{\rm IP}-\left(d_{2}+\frac{1}{2}l_{\rm DX}\right) \theta_{x}^{\rm RP}}{d_{1}-z_{\rm IP}+\frac{1}{2}l_{\rm DX}},\hskip 28.452756pt \xi=\left(1+\frac{\alpha_{0}\left(d_{1}+\frac{1}{2}l_{\rm DX}-z_{\rm IP} \right)}{\theta_{x}^{\rm RP}\left(d_{1}+l_{\rm DX}+d_{2}-z_{\rm IP}\right)-x_ {1}+x_{\rm IP}}\right)^{-1}.\] (B.22)

