## Appendix C Reconstruction of \(\mathbf{m_{TOF}^{2}}\)

**Definitions:**

\begin{tabular}{l l} \(t_{0}\) & - time of the primary \(pp\) interaction, \\ \(t_{i}\) & - time of detection of the hit in TOF by particle \(i=1,2\), \\ \(L_{i}\) & - helical path of the particle \(i=1,2\) from the interaction vertex to the TOF cell with reconstructed hit, \\ \(p_{i}\) & - magnitude of the momentum of particle \(i=1,2\), \\ \(m_{i}\) & - mass of particle \(i=1,2\). \\ \end{tabular}

We consider two particles, which produce two helical tracks in the TPC and produce hits in the TOF detector, see Fig. C.1. The time difference, \(\Delta t\), of their arrivals to the TOF is equal to:

\[\left\{\begin{array}{l}t_{1}-t_{0}=L_{1}\sqrt{1+\frac{m_{1}^{2}}{p_{1}^{2}} },\\ t_{2}-t_{0}=L_{2}\sqrt{1+\frac{m_{2}^{2}}{p_{2}^{2}}}.\end{array}\right.\quad \Rightarrow\quad\Delta t=t_{1}-t_{2}=L_{1}\sqrt{1+\frac{m_{1}^{2}}{p_{1}^{2}} }-L_{2}\sqrt{1+\frac{m_{2}^{2}}{p_{2}^{2}}}.\] (C.1)

Assuming that the particles come from the process of CEP of \(h^{+}h^{-}\) pairs, i.e. they have the same masses, \(m_{1}=m_{2}=m_{\mbox{\tiny TOF}}\), one gets from Eq. (C.1):

\[\mathcal{A}\times\left(m_{\mbox{\tiny TOF}}^{2}\right)^{2}+\mathcal{B}\times m _{\mbox{\tiny TOF}}^{2}+\mathcal{C}=0.\] (C.2)

where

\[\mathcal{A}=-2\frac{L_{1}^{2}L_{2}^{2}}{p_{1}^{2}p_{2}^{2}}+\frac{L_{1}^{4}}{p_ {1}^{4}}+\frac{L_{2}^{4}}{p_{2}^{4}},\] (C.3)

\[\mathcal{B}=-2L_{1}^{2}L_{2}^{2}\left(\frac{1}{p_{1}^{2}}+\frac{1}{p_{2}^{2}} \right)+\frac{2L_{1}^{4}}{p_{1}^{2}}+\frac{2L_{2}^{4}}{p_{2}^{2}}-2\left( \Delta t\right)^{2}\left(\frac{L_{1}^{2}}{p_{1}^{2}}+\frac{L_{2}^{2}}{p_{2}^{ 2}}\right),\] (C.4)

\[\mathcal{C}=\left(\Delta t\right)^{4}-2\left(\Delta t\right)^{2}\left(L_{1}^{ 2}+L_{2}^{2}\right)+L_{1}^{4}+L_{2}^{4}-2L_{1}^{2}L_{2}^{2}.\] (C.5)

Finally1:

Footnote 1: Only one root of Eq. (C.2) is physically meaningful (the one which yields positive \(m_{\mbox{\tiny TOF}}^{2}\)).

\[m_{\mbox{\tiny TOF}}^{2}=\frac{-\mathcal{B}+\sqrt{\mathcal{B}^{2}-4\mathcal{ A}\mathcal{C}}}{2\mathcal{A}}.\] (C.6)

## Appendix D Total RP efficiency

**Definitions:**

\begin{tabular}{l l} RP\({}^{\rm E}\) & - single good quality track (satisfying cuts SC4.1-SC4.2) on the East side, \\ RP\({}^{\rm W}\) & - single good quality track (satisfying cuts SC4.1-SC4.2) on the West side, \\ TR\({}^{\rm E}\) & - trigger signal in the RP branch with the single good track on the East side, \\ TR\({}^{\rm E}_{\rm other}\) & - trigger signal in the RP branch other than the branch with the single good track \\  & on the East side, \\ TR\({}^{\rm W}\) & - trigger signal in the RP branch with the single good track on the West side, \\ TR\({}^{\rm W}_{\rm other}\) & - trigger signal in the RP branch other than the branch with the single good track \\  & on the West side, \\ Veto & - trigger veto on the simultaneous trigger signal in Upper and Lower RP detectors, \\ Veto\({}^{\rm PU}\) & - trigger veto on ET\&IT (Veto) due to pile-up interactions, \\ Veto\({}^{\rm DM}\) & - trigger veto on ET\&IT (Veto) due to forward proton interaction with dead material. \\ \end{tabular}

The total trigger and reconstruction efficiency for tagging of the forward-scattered protons in the CEP events in both the East and the West RP stations can be written as:

\[\begin{split}\varepsilon\left(\text{RP}^{\rm E}\wedge\text{RP}^ {\rm W}\wedge\text{TR}^{\rm E}\wedge\text{TR}^{\rm W}\wedge\text{!Veto}\right)=\\ =\varepsilon\left(\text{RP}^{\rm E}\wedge\text{RP}^{\rm W}\, \middle|\,\text{TR}^{\rm E}\wedge\text{TR}^{\rm W}\wedge\text{!Veto}\right)\cdot\varepsilon\left(\text{TR}^{\rm E}\wedge\text{TR}^{\rm W} \wedge\text{!Veto}\right),\end{split}\] (D.1)

where the factorisation on r.h.s. follows from the definition of conditional probability. Note, that the first term on the r.h.s. represents the reconstruction efficiency of the forward-scattered protons under the condition that the event is triggered, and the second term is just the trigger efficiency.

This first term on the r.h.s. of Eq. (D.1), can be represented as a product of single-proton reconstruction and selection efficiencies described in Sec. 11.2.3 and an additional component, that accounts for the correlation between the East and the West RP's efficiencies. This correlation is defined as:

\[\rho_{\rm EW}=\frac{\varepsilon_{\rm EW}-\varepsilon_{\rm E}\cdot\varepsilon_{ \rm W}}{\sqrt{\varepsilon_{\rm E}\cdot(1-\varepsilon_{\rm E})\cdot \varepsilon_{\rm W}\cdot(1-\varepsilon_{\rm W})}},\] (D.2)

where

\[\varepsilon_{\rm E}=\varepsilon\left(\text{RP}^{\rm E}\,\middle|\,\text{TR}^ {\rm E}\wedge\text{!TR}^{\rm E}_{\rm other}\right),\hskip 28.452756pt\varepsilon_{\rm W }=\varepsilon\left(\text{RP}^{\rm W}\,\middle|\,\text{TR}^{\rm W}\wedge\text{!TR}^{\rm W}_{\rm other}\right),\]

\[\varepsilon_{\rm EW}=\varepsilon\left(\text{RP}^{\rm E}\wedge\text{RP}^{\rm W }\,\middle|\,\text{TR}^{\rm E}\wedge\text{TR}^{\rm W}\wedge\text{!Veto}\right).\]

From the above we get

\[\varepsilon\left(\text{RP}^{\rm E}\wedge\text{RP}^{\rm W}\,\middle|\,\text{TR} ^{\rm E}\wedge\text{TR}^{\rm W}\wedge\text{!Veto}\right)=\varepsilon_{\rm E}\cdot\varepsilon_{\rm W}+\rho_{\rm EW}\cdot \sqrt{\varepsilon_{\rm E}\cdot(1-\varepsilon_{\rm E})\cdot\varepsilon_{\rm W }\cdot(1-\varepsilon_{\rm W})}.\] (D.3)

The correlation coefficient, \(\rho_{\rm EW}\), provides information on simultaneous (un)successful reconstruction/selection of RP track on the East and the West side in the same event. Simultaneous unsuccessful reconstruction of the two forward-scattered protons can be a result of e.g. a pile-up interaction, typically of elastic proton-proton scattering, producing additional tracks/showers simultaneously in the East and the West RPs and thus introducing simultaneous East and West RP inefficiency. With the abovecorrelation taken into account, one is able to properly reconstruct yield of true-level observables and shape of their distributions, as shown in the closure test in Sec. 11.8.2.

In the component of RP efficiency related to the trigger we can use again the conditional probability and factorise it to the part connected with the trigger veto and the efficiency of detecting signals for both forward-scattered protons by the trigger system:

\[\varepsilon\left(\mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{E}}\wedge\mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{W}}\wedge \leavevmode\nobreak\!\mathrm{Veto}\right)=\varepsilon\left(\mathrm{!Veto}\,\big{|}\, \mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{E}}\wedge\mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{W}}\right)\cdot\varepsilon \left(\mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{E}}\wedge\mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{W}}\right).\] (D.4)

The triggering efficiency, \(\varepsilon\left(\mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{E}}\wedge\mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{W}}\right)\), is better than 99% (see Sec. 12.4.3). Efficiency of the (lack of) veto under the condition that the forward-scattered protons were triggered in the East and the West RPs, can be decomposed into an efficiency of the veto induced by a pile-up interaction in the same bunch crossing (\(\mathrm{Veto}^{\mathrm{PU}}\)) and an efficiency of the veto induced by the interaction of the forward-scattered protons with the dead material (\(\mathrm{Veto}^{\mathrm{DM}}\)):

\[\varepsilon\left(\mathrm{!Veto}\,\big{|}\,\mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{E}} \wedge\mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{W}}\right)=\left|\mathrm{Veto}=\mathrm{Veto}^{ \mathrm{PU}}\vee\mathrm{Veto}^{\mathrm{DM}}\right|=\varepsilon\left(\mathrm{! Veto}^{\mathrm{PU}}\wedge\leavevmode\nobreak\!\mathrm{Veto}^{\mathrm{DM}}\,\big{|}\,\mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{E}}\wedge\mathrm{TR }^{\mathrm{W}}\right)=\] (D.5) \[=\varepsilon\left(\mathrm{!Veto}^{\mathrm{DM}}\,\big{|}\,\mathrm{! Veto}^{\mathrm{PU}}\wedge\mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{E}}\wedge\mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{W}} \right)\cdot\varepsilon\left(\mathrm{!Veto}^{\mathrm{PU}}\,\big{|}\,\mathrm{TR }^{\mathrm{E}}\wedge\mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{W}}\right).\]

The first conditional efficiency in the second line of Eq. (D.5) has been already discussed in Sec. 11.1.3 and is equal to the complementary probability to that provided in Eq. (11.3). It can be further factorised into separate efficiencies corresponding to the East and the West RPs, as the probabilities for the forward-scattered protons to induce RP trigger vetoes on the East and one the West sides are independent:

\[\varepsilon\left(\mathrm{!Veto}^{\mathrm{DM}}\,\big{|}\,\mathrm{! Veto}^{\mathrm{PU}}\wedge\mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{E}}\wedge\mathrm{TR}^{ \mathrm{W}}\right)=\] (D.6) \[=\varepsilon^{\mathrm{E}}\left(\mathrm{!Veto}^{\mathrm{DM}}\, \big{|}\,\mathrm{!Veto}^{\mathrm{PU}}\wedge\mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{E}}\wedge \mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{W}}\right)\cdot\varepsilon^{\mathrm{W}}\left(\mathrm{!Veto }^{\mathrm{DM}}\,\big{|}\,\mathrm{!Veto}^{\mathrm{PU}}\wedge\mathrm{TR}^{ \mathrm{E}}\wedge\mathrm{TR}^{\mathrm{W}}\right)\]

The second conditional efficiency in the second line of Eq. (D.5) related with the pile-up is incorporated to overall efficiency of online and offline vetoes described in Sec. 11.3.4 - this is required by the correlation of vetoes, the possibility that vetoes in independent subdetectors take place in the same bunch crossing. This could happen if e.g. single diffraction event occurs on top of the CEP event, yielding a BBC signal and RP signal, both vetoing RP_CPT2 trigger.